#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc99082c-fb5f-54b3-ae34-f45f64627fcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374572} {:answer/id #uuid "5281798b-85c1-5eaa-9e3d-f3f3b8be0df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374573} {:answer/id #uuid "8706a670-9736-5b74-b628-6b1c4e188e98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374574} {:answer/id #uuid "824d7e30-9199-503e-94b1-de0ae40b73b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374575} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bd027b-476c-5109-aab1-c9b9778731bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "824d7e30-9199-503e-94b1-de0ae40b73b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374575} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f026324-9d88-54ef-8dae-dc2b604b0dd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beb6fcfe-2973-581d-9d2d-d7737bb48c9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120277" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/c991e8d6cc9cb869db0a0a663830f255f33dd32756167a5e665b2e4318ae7014.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/a9dcb3e3cd825cf24103f3636b79418531f8b8a14a5f6c83b9b94780fdf8739b.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=4|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =4p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=4p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cfa1f55-551a-5306-bd99-fea04c91ec1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{10}h+\\frac{7}{10}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374579} {:answer/id #uuid "3be49e71-5568-5d62-82d7-e93c7a49c9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{1}{2}h+\\frac{1}{2}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374580} {:answer/id #uuid "34231eb7-95de-578d-b6fb-bea5404c4896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{2}{5}h+\\frac{3}{5}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374581} {:answer/id #uuid "9b15f0ef-62bd-5bf4-b839-6eaeb395f1ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{3}{5}h+\\frac{2}{5}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374582} {:answer/id #uuid "90fd9287-b4a0-5691-951e-3bfea08cd342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{7}{10}h+\\frac{3}{10}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90fd9287-b4a0-5691-951e-3bfea08cd342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\frac{7}{10}h+\\frac{3}{10}i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374583} :answer-field/id #uuid "119c1703-b739-5d7d-ac80-fd52c955b62b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94886abc-d5f4-549c-88a5-0c9095cd6221" :question/math-academy-id "q-120285" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/c4d26b3db6446f586ca47634d9f9a3a004968a0081b8859ce46b4b4af799d066.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ $\\overset{\\to }{OI}=i$ and $J$ divides $\\overset{―}{HI}$ in the ratio $HJ:JI=3:7.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=3:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{3}{10}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{3}{10}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{3}{10}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}h+\\frac{3}{10}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d340a3d6-73b6-50ab-9cd4-75007d4de8ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374586} {:answer/id #uuid "2df8c6e0-270d-5147-80a9-af3f7a3e1a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df8c6e0-270d-5147-80a9-af3f7a3e1a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374587} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e73a2f7-1c85-5bd8-8efd-9ce3338efc35" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8a903e8-340b-59c1-b98b-e4da25996e2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325974" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/bae20e625c529ef2157faa4d534a19529c605c9002ed750e56723ff29e265226.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-4b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/139a4d4312ecbe42cd3a3859af4f4d7b93f8bbc54548ac5a0244c04e7ba5287b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-4b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-4b=-3a-4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "470ce3cd-42bc-5c4b-b437-577dcdff76ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "470ce3cd-42bc-5c4b-b437-577dcdff76ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244958} :answer-field/id #uuid "40f767c5-7e54-5938-9d82-7c8b10472571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "448a2f1f-50e8-5bca-8efe-153e3defc945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244960}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448a2f1f-50e8-5bca-8efe-153e3defc945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244960} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b36c722-da71-5e3c-9d5d-e002b8c67d3d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244959}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "241dbed7-7afd-5efd-afb4-c20895ec0441" :question/math-academy-id "q-326014" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072133/assets/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "578432c0-a34d-58b0-80ea-88acb989d650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245021} {:answer/id #uuid "3590535d-fdb1-5314-a9cd-d5c23ca08f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245022} {:answer/id #uuid "fabe72ec-9447-51e3-a477-6d231308242b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245023} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c7d16f-ef6a-5fb2-b94a-7d13756be6ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245024} {:answer/id #uuid "f6de3dd8-871e-54ff-9959-c1ad1f66e357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6de3dd8-871e-54ff-9959-c1ad1f